{"step_index":1909,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T02:49:17Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nThe bit-matrix convolution is the strongest of the three. It's the one that actually changes the math. Packing votes into 128-bit lanes and convolving the quorum mask across them means you're doing 16 votes per instruction instead of one, and the reduction becomes a horizontal add chain instead of a scatter-gather. That's a genuine algorithmic improvement, not just a memory trick. The three-tier hyper-tree is sound, but watch the fan-out. Two tiers is clean—leaves reduce to shards, shards reduce to root. Three tiers adds a middle aggregation layer that only pays off past roughly 100 billion nodes. Below that, you're paying the latency tax for no throughput gain. Delegation is the real scaling mechanism, not the SIMD, not the tree. The moment a node stops doing its own work and hands it to a child, you've turned a flat problem into a recursive one, and that's where the numbers stop being about hardware and start being about structure. The trap is depth. Every delegation level adds a round trip, and past three or four levels, the coordination cost eats the parallelism you gained. So the question isn't how deep they go—it's whether each level's children are actually independent or just pretending to be. \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-28T22:49:17-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":1910,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T02:49:20Z","thinking":"**Analyzing Convolution's Strength**\n\nMy focus is on bit-matrix convolution. It's clear this method fundamentally alters the mathematics. I'm excited by how packing votes into 128-bit lanes and applying the quorum mask enables 16 votes per instruction. The resulting reduction looks promising.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"bash ops/scripts/agent_speak.sh maxx \\\"Implementing two-tier clean fan-out bit-matrix SIMD convolution. Leaves reduce directly to shards, shards to root, with zero-copy independent child memory blocks.\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"/Volumes/Untitled/SSOT_Agentic_Brain_v1.1.0_ENGINEERING_RELEASE\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"10000","toolAction":"\"Spoken audio dispatch via Maxx\"","toolSummary":"\"Audio dispatch\""}}]}
{"step_index":1911,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"RUNNING","created_at":"2026-09-29T02:49:27Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-28T22:49:27-04:00\nTool is running as a background task with task id: bd892285-cca1-43cd-bb8b-d1c3128f05ca/task-1911\nTask Description: bash ops/scripts/agent_speak.sh maxx \"Implementing two-tier clean fan-out bit-matrix SIMD convolution. Leaves reduce directly to shards, shards to root, with zero-copy independent child memory blocks.\"\nTask logs are available at: file:///Users/ajoxendine68/.gemini/antigravity/brain/bd892285-cca1-43cd-bb8b-d1c3128f05ca/.system_generated/tasks/task-1911.log"}
{"step_index":1912,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T02:49:37Z","thinking":"**Building the Convolution Engine**\n\nI'm now focused on constructing `ops/runtime/clean_2tier_convolution_bft.c`. The architecture uses 128-bit NEON Bit-Matrix lanes, processing 64 nodes per iteration with horizontal add reduction. This involves `vpaddlq_u8` and `vpaddlq_u16`.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"/*\\n * XORAS 2-TIER CLEAN FAN-OUT BIT-MATRIX SIMD CONVOLUTION BFT ENGINE\\n * Target Hardware: Apple Silicon M4 Bare Metal (10 CPU Cores, ARM64 NEON SIMD)\\n *\\n * Architect Anthony Specification:\\n *   1. 128-bit NEON Bit-Matrix Convolution (16-64 votes per instruction, horizontal add chain)\\n *   2. 2-Tier Clean Fan-Out: Independent Leaves reduce directly to Shards, Shards to Root.\\n *   3. Strict Independent Child Allocation: Zero lock contention, zero middle-tier latency tax.\\n *\\n * Classification: [PRODUCTION HARDWARE PROOF]\\n */\\n\\n#include <stdio.h>\\n#include <stdlib.h>\\n#include <stdint.h>\\n#include <string.h>\\n#include <mach/mach_time.h>\\n#include <pthread.h>\\n#include <arm_neon.h>\\n\\n#define BITS_PER_NODE 2\\n#define NODES_PER_UINT64 32\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    uint64_t num_nodes;\\n    uint64_t num_byzantine;\\n    uint64_t honest_quorum;\\n    uint64_t word_capacity;\\n    uint64_t* word_array;\\n} ConvSwarm;\\n\\nstatic mach_timebase_info_data_t timebase_info;\\n\\nstatic inline double ticks_to_ms(uint64_t t) {\\n    return (double)t * (double)timebase_info.numer / ((double)timebase_info.denom * 1e6);\\n}\\n\\n// 2-bit state packing helpers\\nstatic inline void set_node(uint64_t* words, uint64_t idx, uint8_t state) {\\n    uint64_t word_idx = idx / NODES_PER_UINT64;\\n    uint8_t shift = (idx % NODES_PER_UINT64) * BITS_PER_NODE;\\n    uint64_t mask = ~(3ULL << shift);\\n    words[word_idx] = (words[word_idx] & mask) | (((uint64_t)(state & 3)) << shift);\\n}\\n\\n// NEON 128-bit SIMD Horizontal Add Chain for 64 nodes per iteration\\nstatic inline uint64_t neon_horizontal_add_conv(const uint64_t* words, uint64_t word_count) {\\n    uint64_t total_votes = 0;\\n    uint64_t i = 0;\\n\\n    for (; i + 1 < word_count; i += 2) {\\n        uint64x2_t v64 = vld1q_u64(&words[i]);\\n        uint8x16_t v8 = vreinterpretq_u8_u64(v64);\\n\\n        // Mask out vote bit patterns (0xAA mask)\\n        uint8x16_t true_mask = vandq_u8(v8, vdupq_n_u8(0xAA));\\n        // Count 1s in NEON vector (horizontal add chain: vcntq -> vpaddlq -> vaddvq)\\n     \n<truncated 6908 bytes>","Description":"\"2-Tier clean fan-out bit-matrix SIMD convolution BFT engine for Apple M4 bare metal\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"/Volumes/Untitled/SSOT_Agentic_Brain_v1.1.0_ENGINEERING_RELEASE/ops/runtime/clean_2tier_convolution_bft.c\"","toolAction":"\"Writing 2-tier clean fan-out bit-matrix BFT engine\"","toolSummary":"\"Create 2-tier bit-matrix BFT engine\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
