{"step_index":1944,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T02:51:08Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nTwo-tier with zero-copy children is the cleanest version of this — no middle layer, no extra barrier, and each leaf owns its memory block outright so there's nothing to marshal.\n\nThe one thing that decides whether this actually wins: how the shards hand their reduced bitvectors up to root. If that's a copy, you've reintroduced the memory tax you just killed at the leaf level. Keep it a pointer swap or an atomic handoff into a pre-allocated root buffer — same trick, one level up.\n\nAnd the fan-out math: at ten billion nodes across ten cores, each core owns a billion. A billion two-bit votes is two hundred fifty megabytes per core — comfortably inside M4's unified memory, so the leaf convolution stays hot in cache. That's the number that makes this design work instead of just sounding good.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-28T22:51:08-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
